==English==
{{wp|Palooka}}

===Etymology===
{{unk|en|title=Used}} in the [[US]] since the 1920s, originally primarily of boxers. Popularized by Jack Conway of ''[[w:Variety (magazine)|Variety]]'',<ref>''Esquire,'' September 1936</ref><ref>{{w|H. L. Mencken}}, 1945 supplement to ''{{w|The American Language}},'' reviewed in [https://web.archive.org/web/20111221225423/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,792388,00.html Books: Alphabet Soup], ''TIME,'' Monday, Aug. 27, 1945</ref> who also popularized {{m|en|baloney}} and {{m|en|bimbo}}. Further popularized by {{w|Ham Fisher}} in his comic strip ''{{w|Joe Palooka}}'' about a boxer (published in newspapers since 1930, particularly popular in 1940s).<ref>''Cassell’s Dictionary Of Slang.'' Jonathon Green. Cassel & Co., 1998. {{ISBN|0-304-35167-9}}</ref> <!-- Possibly a {{blend|en|Polack|maroon|nocap=1}}. Compare the English word {{m|en|wigger}}. -- SOURCE? Maroon seems irrelevant.
The source is that it's just an assumption based on the word's phonetics and semantics. It could have been influenced by any of the senses of the word. -->

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pəˈluːkə/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-palooka.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s}}

# {{lb|en|US|_|slang}} A [[stupid]], [[oafish]] or [[clumsy]] person.
# {{lb|en|US|boxing|bridge|etc}} Someone [[incompetent]] or [[untalented]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1923|title=w:Lincoln Star|location=Nebraska|month=March
|passage=But [[w:Jack Dempsey|[Jack] Dempsey]] against some '''palooka''' who had been press agented into greatness and into the form of a Dempsey menace — that would pack any outdoor arena.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1994|title=w:Pulp Fiction|writer=Quentin Tarantino; Roger Avary|passage=Butch: Lookin' at somethin', friend?<br/>Vincent: I ain't your friend, '''palooka'''.}}

